
/**
 * @page kernel_console User Console
 *
 * @brief Describes the target-platform user console and its interaction
 *        with the kernel Shell.
 *
 * ## Overview
 *
 * The kernel Shell interacts with a user console through the console
 * interface implemented by the target platform. The console may be
 * implemented using any suitable communication or input device.
 *
 * A UART-based console is the typical implementation, although the
 * kernel does not require a UART or any particular physical interface.
 *
 * The target platform is responsible for providing the console driver
 * and the console service that delivers input to the kernel runtime.
 *
 * ## Console I/O Configuration
 *
 * The target-platform user console implementation is responsible for
 * normalizing terminal input to provide consistent terminal behavior.
 *
 * The following input normalization rules should be applied:
 *
 * - Backspace (BS, @c 0x08): translate BS input to DEL (@c 0x7F)
 *   before delivering it to the kernel Shell.
 * - Delete (DEL, @c 0x7F): upon receiving DEL, perform the terminal
 *   erase sequence Backspace, Space, Backspace (@c 0x08, @c 0x20,
 *   @c 0x08), visually erasing the character preceding the cursor
 *   and moving the cursor one character to the left.
 * - Line endings: normalize CRLF (@c 0x0D 0x0A) sequences to a single
 *   LF (@c 0x0A) character before delivering input to the kernel Shell.
 *
 * These normalization rules provide consistent terminal behavior
 * regardless of the terminal emulator or physical console interface
 * used by the target platform.
 *
 * ## Console Input
 *
 * A typical UART-based implementation consists of the following components:
 *
 * - UART driver - provides low-level UART input operations, such as
 *   polling or retrieving received characters from the UART peripheral.
 * - UART service - polls or retrieves received characters through the
 *   UART driver and publishes user console key events when input is
 *   available.
 * - Kernel Shell - retrieves console key input through the kernel stdio
 *   interface and processes the received input.
 *
 * The UART service publishes @c EVT_USERCON_KEY when a character is
 * received. The event payload contains the received character represented
 * by its code (ASCII in UART terminal alike case) in the console key @c code field.
 *
 * The kernel stdio event subscriber stores the received key and raises
 * @c SIGIO, allowing the Shell to retrieve the key through
 * @ref _kernel_getKey().
 *
 * ## Console Output
 *
 * The target platform implements @ref console.h, which provides the
 * console output interface used by kernel components.
 *
 * A UART-based implementation typically forwards console output
 * operations to the underlying UART driver, which handles the
 * transmission of characters or strings through the UART peripheral.
 *
 * The console interface acts as a platform abstraction between the
 * kernel runtime and the target console implementation.
 *
 * ## Typical UART-Based Implementation
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph console_arch {
 *     rankdir=TB;
 *     node [shape=box, style=rounded];
 *
 *     UART [label="UART Peripheral"];
 *     Driver [label="UART Driver"];
 *     Service [label="UART Service"];
 *     Event [label="EVT_USERCON_KEY\n(code = ASCII character)"];
 *     Stdio [label="Kernel stdio\nEvent subscriber"];
 *     Shell [label="Kernel Shell"];
 *     Console [label="console.h"];
 *
 *     UART -> Driver [label="Receive"];
 *     Driver -> Service [label="Read / poll"];
 *     Service -> Event [label="Publish"];
 *     Event -> Stdio [label="Dispatch"];
 *     Stdio -> Shell [label="SIGIO / key"];
 *
 *     Shell -> Console [label="Output"];
 *     Console -> Driver [label="Transmit"];
 *     Driver -> UART [label="Transmit"];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * ## Example Host Terminal Configuration
 *
 * During development, @c picocom can be used as a host-side terminal
 * to communicate with the target platform over UART.
 *
 * A typical invocation is:
 *
 * @code
 * picocom /dev/{tty_dev} -b {bit_rate} --imap lfcrlf --omap crlf,delbs
 * @endcode
 *
 * Where:
 * - @c /dev/{tty_dev} is the host serial device (e.g. @c /dev/ttyUSB0).
 * - @c {bit_rate} is the UART baud rate configured on the target.
 * - @c --imap @c lfcrlf maps received LF characters to CRLF.
 * - @c --omap @c crlf,delbs maps outgoing LF to CRLF and Backspace
 *   to DEL, respectively.
 *
 * This configuration provides the terminal input/output normalization
 * expected by the target console implementation.
 *
 * @note UART is an illustrative target implementation. Other console
 *       devices and communication interfaces may be used, provided they
 *       implement the required console interface and input event delivery.
 *
 * @see \ref kernel_lite
 * @see \ref events_subscription_publish
 * @see \ref cmd_svc_table
 */
